WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.409 --> 00:00:03.412
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:17.366 --> 00:00:19.243
Yo, hold up.

00:00:25.095 --> 00:00:25.596
What?

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:30.767
Now that's some stupid
shit right there.

00:00:30.767 --> 00:00:31.268
Ouch!

00:00:36.854 --> 00:00:39.648
He's never going to
have any more trouble.

00:00:54.327 --> 00:00:56.495
We didn't land on Plymouth Rock.

00:00:56.495 --> 00:00:59.957
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:02.543 --> 00:01:04.000
Asalamalakim

00:01:14.815 --> 00:01:15.569
Murder!

00:01:15.569 --> 00:01:16.367
Murder!

00:01:17.963 --> 00:01:19.630
I have them.

00:01:28.983 --> 00:01:30.985
You must be Black dynamite.

00:01:31.155 --> 00:01:33.126
All in favor, raise your hand.

00:01:40.584 --> 00:01:41.594
We he at?

00:01:45.342 --> 00:01:48.685
Respect, if you can't
do that, then go.

00:01:58.981 --> 00:01:59.982
Now get on back to work.

00:02:13.612 --> 00:02:14.237
Demonstrate.

00:02:14.237 --> 00:02:15.084
Resist.

00:02:19.333 --> 00:02:21.345
They're going to
kill my children.

00:02:26.255 --> 00:02:29.675
That means march, that
means disturb the peace.

00:02:35.334 --> 00:02:36.626
Please.

00:02:40.519 --> 00:02:42.114
He better come quick.

00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:47.943
We ain't slaves no more.

00:02:52.459 --> 00:02:55.418
I am loyal to the throne.

00:02:55.418 --> 00:02:56.377
For the tribe!

00:03:01.832 --> 00:03:02.332
What's up.

00:03:07.501 --> 00:03:08.543
We are vegetarians.

00:03:17.973 --> 00:03:20.366
And that's the truth Ruth.

00:03:36.799 --> 00:03:37.926
Hello.

00:03:37.926 --> 00:03:43.681
I am Ruth Carter costume
designer to the stars.

00:03:43.681 --> 00:03:50.730
But I did start a long time
ago and when I was in college.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:56.236
I decided that I wanted to
be a costume designer, when

00:03:56.236 --> 00:03:58.613
I tried out for a play.

00:03:58.613 --> 00:04:02.867
And I didn't make the
audition, so my teacher

00:04:02.867 --> 00:04:05.995
who was the director
of the play asked me

00:04:05.995 --> 00:04:08.373
if I wanted to do the costumes?

00:04:08.373 --> 00:04:12.210
And I said yes, I
said yes because I

00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:16.839
had been toying around with
the sewing machine in my room

00:04:16.839 --> 00:04:18.341
as I grew up.

00:04:18.341 --> 00:04:20.885
And I also loved to draw.

00:04:20.885 --> 00:04:24.430
So I went to the
library and I looked up

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:27.850
what a costume designer does.

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:31.354
And it fit who I felt I was.

00:04:31.354 --> 00:04:33.940
And so my journey began.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:38.653
Here I am sitting
on stage in college.

00:04:38.653 --> 00:04:42.156
I really loved being a thespian.

00:04:42.156 --> 00:04:46.536
I enjoyed, I enjoyed
the stage so much.

00:04:46.536 --> 00:04:48.454
I wanted to be an actress.

00:04:48.454 --> 00:04:53.001
And I'm in rehearsal
here, probably not

00:04:53.001 --> 00:04:58.172
Unlike some of you who enjoy
going through characterization.

00:04:58.172 --> 00:05:04.345
And the artistic development of
a character finding their arc.

00:05:04.345 --> 00:05:08.349
And immersing
yourself in character.

00:05:08.349 --> 00:05:13.896
I believe that my work as an
artist in college, actually

00:05:13.896 --> 00:05:16.482
really helped me quite
a bit to understand

00:05:16.482 --> 00:05:19.360
the journey of an actor.

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:22.322
Here I am on the set
of Black Panther.

00:05:22.322 --> 00:05:25.325
I'm standing next to the
director Ryan Coogler.

00:05:25.325 --> 00:05:29.954
And we are examining M'Baku
the leader of the Jabari tribe

00:05:29.954 --> 00:05:35.752
who is about to appear on
camera for the first time.

00:05:35.752 --> 00:05:41.090
It's wonderful to be able to
present culture and character

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:44.052
in the form of costume design.

00:05:44.052 --> 00:05:49.349
And M'Baku we will discover
through this lecture

00:05:49.349 --> 00:05:52.977
how he came about with
our inspiration coming

00:05:52.977 --> 00:05:55.480
from the Dogon tribe.

00:05:55.480 --> 00:06:00.151
But here I am working away
in one of the many studios

00:06:00.151 --> 00:06:03.404
that I put together for film.

00:06:03.404 --> 00:06:07.742
And many times they're never
really big glamorous places.

00:06:07.742 --> 00:06:13.790
They're cramped, small spaces
that we have to create in

00:06:13.790 --> 00:06:18.252
and the environments
sometimes aren't optimum,

00:06:18.252 --> 00:06:22.048
but what is consistent is
the love for storytelling.

00:06:22.048 --> 00:06:25.760
And storytelling is
really the difference

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:30.139
between fashion and costume.

00:06:30.139 --> 00:06:36.938
And costume is really the
art of creating a character

00:06:36.938 --> 00:06:39.941
through garments,
but also the art

00:06:39.941 --> 00:06:45.238
of creating a garment
that tells a story.

00:06:45.238 --> 00:06:50.159
Many people think that I became
a costume designer because

00:06:50.159 --> 00:06:56.833
of Dior, fashion
design, Barbie, but it

00:06:56.833 --> 00:06:58.292
was none of those things.

00:06:58.292 --> 00:07:03.339
Actually it was James
Baldwin, Lorraine Hansberry,

00:07:03.339 --> 00:07:08.219
Sonia Sanchez, Nikki Giovanni,
those were my mentors.

00:07:08.219 --> 00:07:10.430
Those were my inspirations.

00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:15.810
Their stories told such
vivid tales of Black culture.

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:18.771
I was enthralled by them.

00:07:18.771 --> 00:07:23.776
And I really wanted to bring
these characters to life.

00:07:23.776 --> 00:07:26.112
One day in the 1980s--

00:07:26.112 --> 00:07:31.659
and 1987 I met a
guy named Spike Lee.

00:07:31.659 --> 00:07:37.165
And he called me early one
morning and he said "Ruth?"

00:07:37.165 --> 00:07:38.166
"Hello?"

00:07:38.166 --> 00:07:39.083
"Ruth?"

00:07:39.083 --> 00:07:40.418
"Hello?"

00:07:40.418 --> 00:07:43.212
"RUTH" "Yes?"

00:07:43.212 --> 00:07:49.385
"This is the man of your
dreams" I said "Denzel?"

00:07:49.385 --> 00:07:50.011
He said "No.

00:07:50.011 --> 00:07:54.724
This is Spike Lee, and I want
you to do my next film school

00:07:54.724 --> 00:07:56.225
days."

00:07:56.225 --> 00:07:59.020
So I moved to
Brooklyn, New York.

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:02.607
And I started to
design and create

00:08:02.607 --> 00:08:06.444
the costumes for my
first film school days.

00:08:06.444 --> 00:08:08.696
Now I had done so much theater.

00:08:08.696 --> 00:08:11.073
I knew how to create characters.

00:08:11.073 --> 00:08:13.701
I knew the character arcs.

00:08:13.701 --> 00:08:17.246
But I had never built
this many costumes.

00:08:17.246 --> 00:08:19.457
And these many
characters before.

00:08:19.457 --> 00:08:22.168
So it was a big task for me.

00:08:22.168 --> 00:08:27.173
And I cherish the experience.

00:08:27.173 --> 00:08:31.135
And this is one of our
sketches from school days.

00:08:31.135 --> 00:08:33.679
I went to my brother
who is an artist

00:08:33.679 --> 00:08:36.766
and I asked him how to move
through these characters

00:08:36.766 --> 00:08:37.725
on campus?

00:08:37.725 --> 00:08:40.561
How to move through the
actual design process

00:08:40.561 --> 00:08:42.230
as quickly as possible?

00:08:42.230 --> 00:08:48.361
And he guided my
artistic presentation.

00:08:48.361 --> 00:08:51.280
I went on to do so many
films with Spike Lee,

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:55.701
do the right thing where we
use the commutative of Brooklyn

00:08:55.701 --> 00:09:00.581
to portray the protest
film to do the right thing.

00:09:00.581 --> 00:09:02.917
And all of the elements.

00:09:02.917 --> 00:09:07.630
This is the late Bill Nunn
who is wearing a big star

00:09:07.630 --> 00:09:12.051
or die t-shirt, that we had
painted by a local artist.

00:09:12.051 --> 00:09:16.222
So it meant a lot to us
to involve the community,

00:09:16.222 --> 00:09:19.475
in our community story.

00:09:19.475 --> 00:09:23.104
I also did the life
story of Tina Turner.

00:09:23.104 --> 00:09:26.190
And worked with Angela
Bassett quite a bit.

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:28.734
And telling that
story with Angela

00:09:28.734 --> 00:09:31.237
was a memory I'll never forget.

00:09:31.237 --> 00:09:33.864
Because she immersed
herself in character.

00:09:33.864 --> 00:09:38.160
And I immersed myself
in costume design.

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:45.334
I went on to design the story
of Malcolm X. And on Malcolm X

00:09:45.334 --> 00:09:47.795
I received an Oscar nomination.

00:09:47.795 --> 00:09:52.091
And this was a transformation
for Denzel Washington, who

00:09:52.091 --> 00:10:00.141
went from a ballroom
dancer, wearing zoot suits

00:10:00.141 --> 00:10:02.935
to a national speaker.

00:10:02.935 --> 00:10:06.314
And it was really
important for us

00:10:06.314 --> 00:10:11.152
to portray his life as
honestly, and as authentically,

00:10:11.152 --> 00:10:11.694
as we could.

00:10:11.694 --> 00:10:15.990
We had purpose when
we did our research.

00:10:15.990 --> 00:10:19.368
And we went through
this process.

00:10:19.368 --> 00:10:22.371
I also did a film
called Amistad.

00:10:22.371 --> 00:10:25.750
And that was directed
by Steven Spielberg.

00:10:25.750 --> 00:10:31.213
It told the story of
the Amistad Africans

00:10:31.213 --> 00:10:33.758
who were on one of
the last ships that

00:10:33.758 --> 00:10:37.970
sailed from Africa to Virginia.

00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:40.514
And they took over the ship.

00:10:40.514 --> 00:10:43.059
And it was a long court case.

00:10:43.059 --> 00:10:48.522
But I really wanted to
show them in their native,

00:10:48.522 --> 00:10:54.820
in their true sense, because
they were not slaves.

00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:56.322
They were Africans.

00:10:56.322 --> 00:11:01.160
So I looked up the culture
and I saw that many of them

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:02.787
would have been Muslim.

00:11:02.787 --> 00:11:07.166
So we wrapped their
hair, and we gave them

00:11:07.166 --> 00:11:10.378
I felt things that
would tell us more

00:11:10.378 --> 00:11:12.171
about where they came from.

00:11:14.840 --> 00:11:18.928
And moving on it
was 10 years maybe

00:11:18.928 --> 00:11:23.849
more I think 20 years
later that I won the Oscar

00:11:23.849 --> 00:11:25.768
for Black Panther.

00:11:25.768 --> 00:11:31.440
So I want to take you a little
bit through that journey.

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:33.317
We created Wakanda.

00:11:33.317 --> 00:11:37.363
And I'm sure many of
you have heard of it?

00:11:37.363 --> 00:11:40.449
It sounds a little like
Uganda, but it's not Uganda,

00:11:40.449 --> 00:11:41.701
it's Wakanda.

00:11:41.701 --> 00:11:46.914
And there it sits in the
northern part of Africa.

00:11:46.914 --> 00:11:50.376
It's right there East
of Mali, and North

00:11:50.376 --> 00:11:53.379
of Nigeria, South of Libya.

00:11:53.379 --> 00:11:56.716
But I have to tell
you one true fact,

00:11:56.716 --> 00:11:59.552
and that is it doesn't exist.

00:11:59.552 --> 00:12:02.430
You can't take a plane
there, you can't go there,

00:12:02.430 --> 00:12:04.056
it doesn't exist.

00:12:04.056 --> 00:12:07.143
But it was made up by
Stan Lee and Jack Kirby

00:12:07.143 --> 00:12:12.440
back in the 1960s when there
was a lot of civil unrest.

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:14.859
The Civil rights
movement was in effect.

00:12:14.859 --> 00:12:19.029
And they felt like they
wanted to create a superhero

00:12:19.029 --> 00:12:22.283
for the Black community.

00:12:22.283 --> 00:12:26.370
So how did we come up with
the stories of Wakanda?

00:12:26.370 --> 00:12:30.249
How did we come up with
the people of Wakanda?

00:12:30.249 --> 00:12:33.461
Well, we studied so many
of the wonderful tribes

00:12:33.461 --> 00:12:34.795
throughout Africa.

00:12:34.795 --> 00:12:36.297
There's thousands of them.

00:12:36.297 --> 00:12:42.595
And we picked just a few to
be inspired by our tribes

00:12:42.595 --> 00:12:45.055
in Africa, in Wakanda.

00:12:45.055 --> 00:12:46.807
And we gave them names.

00:12:46.807 --> 00:12:48.851
One would be the border tribe.

00:12:48.851 --> 00:12:51.771
And they were inspired
by the Lesotho people

00:12:51.771 --> 00:12:55.357
of South Africa who wear
the wonderful blankets,

00:12:55.357 --> 00:12:57.651
and the heart shaped hats.

00:12:57.651 --> 00:13:01.989
And they are some of this
Wakanda's best warriors.

00:13:01.989 --> 00:13:08.162
And the first line of defense
against the outside world.

00:13:08.162 --> 00:13:10.915
Here they are in their
beautiful blankets that

00:13:10.915 --> 00:13:14.293
were given to them by the King.

00:13:14.293 --> 00:13:17.129
Well, our director
felt that he wanted

00:13:17.129 --> 00:13:19.799
to use these blankets
in a special way.

00:13:19.799 --> 00:13:22.676
And in that way he
wanted to create

00:13:22.676 --> 00:13:27.264
a shield out of these
blankets by putting vibranium

00:13:27.264 --> 00:13:28.432
on one side.

00:13:28.432 --> 00:13:32.478
How many of you have
heard of vibranium?

00:13:32.478 --> 00:13:35.064
Well, we did some illustrations.

00:13:35.064 --> 00:13:39.985
And in those illustrations we
showed different ways, in which

00:13:39.985 --> 00:13:43.447
we could adorn those blankets.

00:13:43.447 --> 00:13:45.866
And this illustration
is somewhat

00:13:45.866 --> 00:13:48.452
of a community and a family.

00:13:48.452 --> 00:13:51.539
We have our tribal
elder in the front,

00:13:51.539 --> 00:13:56.126
our Queen Mother to his
right, our fierce warriors

00:13:56.126 --> 00:14:00.172
on the ends, and the
general community.

00:14:00.172 --> 00:14:02.842
But when we looked at
these illustrations,

00:14:02.842 --> 00:14:07.096
we thought this doesn't look
like a forward thinking nation.

00:14:07.096 --> 00:14:14.061
This looks like more ancient and
more as they were in the past.

00:14:14.061 --> 00:14:19.483
And since we were creating a
forward, more modern Wakanda,

00:14:19.483 --> 00:14:23.320
we decided to do more
illustrations that would

00:14:23.320 --> 00:14:26.031
incorporate some new ideas.

00:14:26.031 --> 00:14:28.200
And we did that with
all of the tribes

00:14:28.200 --> 00:14:31.829
that we were inspired
by to tell the story.

00:14:31.829 --> 00:14:34.290
Here we have the merchant tribe.

00:14:34.290 --> 00:14:39.628
The merchant tribe is inspired
by the Tuareg of Mali.

00:14:39.628 --> 00:14:44.633
And where you see that the
women do not cover their faces,

00:14:44.633 --> 00:14:45.926
and the men do.

00:14:45.926 --> 00:14:52.474
So that makes our tribal elder
here in the front a woman.

00:14:52.474 --> 00:14:55.561
We did the same
exercise by showing

00:14:55.561 --> 00:15:00.274
how modern the Tuareg or the
merchant tribe I should say

00:15:00.274 --> 00:15:02.484
modernized their look.

00:15:02.484 --> 00:15:05.529
And here's how it plays
out at the Warrior falls.

00:15:05.529 --> 00:15:10.910
You see our tribal elder Connie
with her large filani earrings.

00:15:10.910 --> 00:15:16.540
And she's standing next
to her fierce Warrior.

00:15:16.540 --> 00:15:19.084
We did the same thing
with the mining tribe.

00:15:19.084 --> 00:15:22.296
And they were inspired
by the Samburu

00:15:22.296 --> 00:15:26.800
people of northern Kenya,
the Dinka of South Sudan,

00:15:26.800 --> 00:15:31.889
the Himba of Namibia,
the Maasai of Kenya.

00:15:31.889 --> 00:15:35.184
You see Shuri, the
Panthers little sister,

00:15:35.184 --> 00:15:43.651
she's wearing beads and corsets
that we fashioned by the Dinka.

00:15:46.570 --> 00:15:50.824
And here is how the miners
looked in our illustration.

00:15:50.824 --> 00:15:53.869
We wanted to come up
with ways in which they

00:15:53.869 --> 00:15:57.289
would be mining vibranium.

00:15:57.289 --> 00:16:01.460
How many have you heard, have
you have heard of vibranium?

00:16:01.460 --> 00:16:06.215
Yes, vibranium is the strongest
metal known in the world.

00:16:06.215 --> 00:16:10.761
And it's only found, in
Wakanda - all made up.

00:16:10.761 --> 00:16:13.681
But we have our tribal
elder in the middle.

00:16:13.681 --> 00:16:16.642
And we have the Queen
Mother of the mining

00:16:16.642 --> 00:16:20.229
tribe standing to his side.

00:16:20.229 --> 00:16:23.816
Here's how it played out
at the Warrior falls.

00:16:23.816 --> 00:16:28.529
Now we begin to mix
other tribal inspirations

00:16:28.529 --> 00:16:31.657
into the idea of the miners.

00:16:31.657 --> 00:16:34.410
And the miners wear
this vivid orange.

00:16:34.410 --> 00:16:37.496
They also - Our
tribal elder also

00:16:37.496 --> 00:16:40.916
wears a Himba style hairstyle.

00:16:40.916 --> 00:16:45.629
And that's the Shea butter
and the rich clay soil

00:16:45.629 --> 00:16:48.841
mixed and adorned in the hair.

00:16:48.841 --> 00:16:52.386
Some people think
it's worn that way

00:16:52.386 --> 00:16:57.516
as they put the shea butter on
their skin and on their bodies.

00:16:57.516 --> 00:17:01.854
And people think it's
for antiseptic reasons,

00:17:01.854 --> 00:17:03.439
because of long droughts.

00:17:03.439 --> 00:17:06.316
Or to ward off mosquitoes.

00:17:06.316 --> 00:17:08.027
It's none of those things.

00:17:08.027 --> 00:17:09.570
It's just beauty.

00:17:09.570 --> 00:17:11.864
They love the way
they look that way.

00:17:11.864 --> 00:17:14.950
And I think it's outstanding.

00:17:14.950 --> 00:17:18.704
Standing next to Connie
is her fierce Warrior

00:17:18.704 --> 00:17:21.874
who's inspired by the Maasai.

00:17:21.874 --> 00:17:24.626
Now if you follow
the comics, you'll

00:17:24.626 --> 00:17:28.172
know that at tribal
council there is

00:17:28.172 --> 00:17:31.467
a elder in a Maasai headdress.

00:17:31.467 --> 00:17:33.802
Very large Maasai headdress.

00:17:33.802 --> 00:17:37.598
And I was prepared to put
this look in tribal council.

00:17:37.598 --> 00:17:40.768
But Ryan our director,
didn't want it there.

00:17:40.768 --> 00:17:45.522
He actually wanted the man
with the lip plate instead.

00:17:45.522 --> 00:17:48.650
So when we got to
Warrior falls, I

00:17:48.650 --> 00:17:51.487
felt like there was an
opportunity for me to put

00:17:51.487 --> 00:17:53.405
the Maasai headdress there.

00:17:53.405 --> 00:17:56.408
So I went to Pier One
imports, and I bought

00:17:56.408 --> 00:17:59.703
myself a beaded placemat.

00:17:59.703 --> 00:18:01.997
And I cut a hole in it.

00:18:01.997 --> 00:18:05.084
And then painted it
and we adorned it

00:18:05.084 --> 00:18:09.880
with feathers, and fur, and
we got our Maasai headdress

00:18:09.880 --> 00:18:14.927
in the film, for about 20 bucks.

00:18:14.927 --> 00:18:16.762
Here is the Golden tribe.

00:18:16.762 --> 00:18:19.515
And the Golden tribe
is inspired by the Zulu

00:18:19.515 --> 00:18:22.267
tribe of South Africa.

00:18:22.267 --> 00:18:25.687
The most powerful
tribe of Wakanda.

00:18:25.687 --> 00:18:29.274
Their technology has
developed far ahead

00:18:29.274 --> 00:18:31.568
of the rest of the world.

00:18:31.568 --> 00:18:35.948
And it's the tribe
of the Royal family.

00:18:35.948 --> 00:18:39.952
Here's how we started
our illustration

00:18:39.952 --> 00:18:44.832
based on some of the tribal
customs, and celebratory looks.

00:18:44.832 --> 00:18:48.168
We have our tribal elder
and our Queen Mother.

00:18:48.168 --> 00:18:53.382
And then we modernized the
look, just so we could give them

00:18:53.382 --> 00:18:57.469
a look where we're not showing
Wakanda as someplace that

00:18:57.469 --> 00:18:58.929
doesn't move ahead.

00:18:58.929 --> 00:19:02.224
Wakanda is a place that
moves forward and has

00:19:02.224 --> 00:19:05.185
great technology.

00:19:05.185 --> 00:19:06.645
The Artisian tribe.

00:19:06.645 --> 00:19:10.315
The Artisian tribe were
inspired by the two Turkana

00:19:10.315 --> 00:19:12.025
people of Kenya.

00:19:12.025 --> 00:19:14.653
The most traditional
of all the tribes

00:19:14.653 --> 00:19:20.075
responsible for pottery,
textiles, and the arts.

00:19:20.075 --> 00:19:24.913
So how could you not use as
much of the beautiful beading

00:19:24.913 --> 00:19:30.502
and artistry that surrounds
many places in Africa

00:19:30.502 --> 00:19:33.755
to create this look
for these tribes?

00:19:33.755 --> 00:19:38.719
And you notice we have also
added the modern looks.

00:19:38.719 --> 00:19:40.137
There is the River tribe.

00:19:40.137 --> 00:19:42.431
The River tribe was
based on the Tsamai

00:19:42.431 --> 00:19:46.810
and the Suri of Ethiopia,
and the Wagenya of The Congo.

00:19:46.810 --> 00:19:52.608
So we combined many inspirations
from around the central and

00:19:52.608 --> 00:19:54.568
southern part of Africa.

00:19:54.568 --> 00:19:58.405
And we really did love the
look of the Suri children,

00:19:58.405 --> 00:20:04.328
and how they playfully adorn
their hair and their faces.

00:20:04.328 --> 00:20:09.499
We also modernized and took
a few artistic liberties

00:20:09.499 --> 00:20:11.752
with the Wagenya look.

00:20:11.752 --> 00:20:13.962
And the stick
fighters I was just

00:20:13.962 --> 00:20:17.799
enthralled by some of the ways
that they adorn themselves

00:20:17.799 --> 00:20:23.680
with armor and in
really unique ways.

00:20:23.680 --> 00:20:29.728
Nakia who is played by Lupita
Nyong'o is from the River

00:20:29.728 --> 00:20:30.938
tribe.

00:20:30.938 --> 00:20:34.274
We presented to looks for Nakia.

00:20:34.274 --> 00:20:39.780
On your right see she's
inspired by the Hammer tribe.

00:20:39.780 --> 00:20:43.784
With the skins that are
beaded and the cowrie shells

00:20:43.784 --> 00:20:45.410
that go around the neck.

00:20:45.410 --> 00:20:47.371
Really beautiful look.

00:20:47.371 --> 00:20:50.415
And there on the
left you see Nakia

00:20:50.415 --> 00:20:52.793
in a totally made
up costume that

00:20:52.793 --> 00:20:57.381
does have many elements
from the Suri children.

00:20:57.381 --> 00:21:00.384
And that's the one
we chose to create.

00:21:00.384 --> 00:21:03.971
Here's how it played out
at the Warrior falls.

00:21:03.971 --> 00:21:08.600
You see Nakia adorning
her Warrior look

00:21:08.600 --> 00:21:10.727
based on the Suri children.

00:21:10.727 --> 00:21:15.315
And next to her is her
tribal elder with complete

00:21:15.315 --> 00:21:16.817
with his lip plate.

00:21:16.817 --> 00:21:22.030
And his costume is
completely made up.

00:21:22.030 --> 00:21:25.158
And so we get to
our Royal family.

00:21:25.158 --> 00:21:30.330
And we see them all together
in a photograph like this.

00:21:30.330 --> 00:21:32.833
We took this photograph
during shooting.

00:21:32.833 --> 00:21:37.462
And it was the first time that
the whole cast got together

00:21:37.462 --> 00:21:39.798
in one place in costume.

00:21:39.798 --> 00:21:42.592
And this was an
exciting day for me.

00:21:42.592 --> 00:21:44.845
I looked to my left
and to my right

00:21:44.845 --> 00:21:47.848
and I wondered if
anybody else was excited

00:21:47.848 --> 00:21:52.311
as I was about costume
design and storytelling.

00:21:52.311 --> 00:21:55.939
In this photograph,
you see Zuri in purple.

00:21:55.939 --> 00:21:59.234
And he is the tribal priest.

00:21:59.234 --> 00:22:02.362
He is the high
priestess of Wakanda.

00:22:02.362 --> 00:22:06.199
And he adorns many of
the tribal language

00:22:06.199 --> 00:22:08.201
that we put in the costume.

00:22:08.201 --> 00:22:12.622
He has mali, he has neck
rings, he has a tabard.

00:22:12.622 --> 00:22:18.378
Next to Zuri is what W'kabi
the leader of the border tribe.

00:22:18.378 --> 00:22:20.922
And he's adorning
the Lesotho blanket.

00:22:20.922 --> 00:22:25.344
And his chest piece that
is made of vibranium.

00:22:25.344 --> 00:22:28.263
Above W'kabi is Killmonger.

00:22:28.263 --> 00:22:31.767
Killmonger who
returned to Wakanda

00:22:31.767 --> 00:22:36.480
to defend his rightful,
rightful place to the throne.

00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:39.066
Below Killmonger is Nakia.

00:22:39.066 --> 00:22:42.277
Nakia with her ring
blades and her and she

00:22:42.277 --> 00:22:45.655
is the fierce Warrior
of the River tribe.

00:22:45.655 --> 00:22:48.533
Below Nakia is Okoye.

00:22:48.533 --> 00:22:53.121
Okoye is the leader of the
Dora Milaje the general.

00:22:53.121 --> 00:22:58.919
The highest ranking fighting
force, female fighting force

00:22:58.919 --> 00:23:00.754
of Wakanda.

00:23:00.754 --> 00:23:05.550
Below Okoye is Shuri -the little
sister of the Black Panther,

00:23:05.550 --> 00:23:09.054
and the leader of the
Wakanda design group.

00:23:09.054 --> 00:23:13.058
She actually designs the suit.

00:23:13.058 --> 00:23:16.269
Above Shuri is queen Ramonda.

00:23:16.269 --> 00:23:18.688
The Queen Mother of Wakanda.

00:23:18.688 --> 00:23:23.610
And she's standing next to the
late great Chadwick Boseman,

00:23:23.610 --> 00:23:24.694
as the Black Panther.

00:23:27.197 --> 00:23:30.200
So let's quickly talk
about some costume design.

00:23:33.370 --> 00:23:37.207
Here we have W'kabi in
his Lesotho blanket.

00:23:37.207 --> 00:23:40.419
These are the illustrations
that are done for costume design

00:23:40.419 --> 00:23:42.295
early in the process.

00:23:42.295 --> 00:23:47.217
We present all of these
costumes to Marvel.

00:23:47.217 --> 00:23:49.428
We present these sketches.

00:23:49.428 --> 00:23:51.346
And they look
through them and they

00:23:51.346 --> 00:23:54.433
decide, which costumes
they like the best

00:23:54.433 --> 00:23:56.560
and which ones
we're going to make.

00:23:56.560 --> 00:23:59.229
You can see on
W'kabi's blanket, we

00:23:59.229 --> 00:24:05.444
have illustrated vibranium in
the form of letters and things

00:24:05.444 --> 00:24:09.364
on the inside portion
of his blanket.

00:24:09.364 --> 00:24:11.950
Here you see the
palace guards, you

00:24:11.950 --> 00:24:16.413
see another design
for the Dora Milaje,

00:24:16.413 --> 00:24:20.125
you see a design for Shuri.

00:24:20.125 --> 00:24:22.461
Well, let's begin
Black Panther suit.

00:24:22.461 --> 00:24:26.882
When I first saw the suit
that was presented in Captain

00:24:26.882 --> 00:24:32.888
America Civil War, I thought to
myself, it was on a mannequin,

00:24:32.888 --> 00:24:36.183
it was in my office just stiff.

00:24:36.183 --> 00:24:40.145
And I thought this is
not what I expected

00:24:40.145 --> 00:24:43.356
from this the Black
Panther costume.

00:24:43.356 --> 00:24:46.860
I expect a little
bit more something.

00:24:46.860 --> 00:24:50.864
And so I asked Chadwick
Boseman to come in my office

00:24:50.864 --> 00:24:52.657
and put the suit on.

00:24:52.657 --> 00:24:54.159
And he did.

00:24:54.159 --> 00:24:57.120
And when he put on
that suit, and he

00:24:57.120 --> 00:25:03.543
began to stretch and do his
karate stances, I was amazed.

00:25:03.543 --> 00:25:07.005
It really had magic.

00:25:07.005 --> 00:25:12.135
When he put on that and
that the mask and it became

00:25:12.135 --> 00:25:15.430
the Black Panther, it was
no longer Chadwick Boseman.

00:25:15.430 --> 00:25:17.682
I was in awe.

00:25:17.682 --> 00:25:19.559
And then Chadwick
said well, you know

00:25:19.559 --> 00:25:22.562
I can't breathe out of
my nose in this helmet.

00:25:22.562 --> 00:25:28.068
And I can't lift my arm
above a certain height.

00:25:28.068 --> 00:25:29.653
So I thought that
would never do.

00:25:29.653 --> 00:25:34.866
So we went on to build
the Black Panther costume.

00:25:34.866 --> 00:25:38.870
And we pay particular
attention to the areas

00:25:38.870 --> 00:25:40.664
of the face and the helmet.

00:25:40.664 --> 00:25:42.749
So that he could
actually breathe.

00:25:42.749 --> 00:25:46.002
And he could actually
lift his arm.

00:25:46.002 --> 00:25:48.964
It started with a
muscle sculpt where

00:25:48.964 --> 00:25:52.634
we create the muscles for
the Black Panther suit.

00:25:52.634 --> 00:25:56.221
We vacuform them.

00:25:56.221 --> 00:26:01.893
And we paint them silver so
they represent vibranium.

00:26:01.893 --> 00:26:04.020
But there was one
particular thing

00:26:04.020 --> 00:26:07.941
that we did to the Panther
suit that was really special.

00:26:07.941 --> 00:26:11.444
And that was we created
the surface texture

00:26:11.444 --> 00:26:14.614
in the form of a triangle.

00:26:14.614 --> 00:26:20.537
And for us the triangle was like
a sacred geometry of Africa.

00:26:20.537 --> 00:26:26.918
It's a shape that you see
in so much of the artistry.

00:26:26.918 --> 00:26:30.046
We called it the
Okavango triangle.

00:26:30.046 --> 00:26:33.508
Named after the
long Okavango river.

00:26:33.508 --> 00:26:39.055
And the triangle had a meaning
for us that was of the family.

00:26:39.055 --> 00:26:44.311
The father, the
mother, and the child.

00:26:44.311 --> 00:26:48.231
We also had to come up with
different ideas for King

00:26:48.231 --> 00:26:54.738
T'Challa to wear as he moved
around the city of Wakanda

00:26:54.738 --> 00:26:57.282
and ruled over Wakanda.

00:26:57.282 --> 00:26:59.909
And so here we have
what was inspired

00:26:59.909 --> 00:27:03.121
by the Nigerian senators suits.

00:27:03.121 --> 00:27:08.376
And the Nigerian senator suit
has the wonderful embroidery

00:27:08.376 --> 00:27:10.462
around the neck
and down the front.

00:27:10.462 --> 00:27:14.049
It's usually a tunic and a
pair of very simple pants.

00:27:14.049 --> 00:27:16.801
But we put the embroidery
around the tailcoat,

00:27:16.801 --> 00:27:20.388
just to give him a little
bit more formality.

00:27:20.388 --> 00:27:25.018
We also created the
crown for Queen Ramonda.

00:27:25.018 --> 00:27:28.438
Not in the comics queen Rmonda
does where the Isicholo.

00:27:28.438 --> 00:27:31.149
The Isicholo is
the married woman

00:27:31.149 --> 00:27:33.401
to South African
married woman hat

00:27:33.401 --> 00:27:36.071
as you see in red on your left.

00:27:36.071 --> 00:27:40.617
So we created this design and
then we decided to 3D print it

00:27:40.617 --> 00:27:42.452
So it would be perfect.

00:27:42.452 --> 00:27:48.416
We needed the Queen's crown to
be a perfect sphere that maybe

00:27:48.416 --> 00:27:52.170
had some folklore that the
center faced the North,

00:27:52.170 --> 00:27:55.215
that the sides faced East-West.

00:27:55.215 --> 00:27:58.510
And it really meant
a lot to me that we

00:27:58.510 --> 00:28:01.513
would use this technology
that was forward

00:28:01.513 --> 00:28:03.807
thinking for the queen.

00:28:03.807 --> 00:28:08.728
We also 3D printed
her shoulder mantle.

00:28:08.728 --> 00:28:10.438
Now the shoulder
mantle you see here

00:28:10.438 --> 00:28:14.943
in the colors is how many
pieces are printed in the big 3D

00:28:14.943 --> 00:28:16.319
printing machine.

00:28:16.319 --> 00:28:18.446
And it comes to
us in many pieces.

00:28:18.446 --> 00:28:21.157
And we take it to our workshop.

00:28:21.157 --> 00:28:26.413
And we glue it together and
then we actually paint it.

00:28:26.413 --> 00:28:29.666
So here's how it
looked in our film.

00:28:29.666 --> 00:28:32.502
On your left you see she
was standing completely

00:28:32.502 --> 00:28:36.131
in blue screen, but in
the movie on your right

00:28:36.131 --> 00:28:40.009
see she's standing in Wakanda.

00:28:40.009 --> 00:28:42.679
So I told you a little
bit about the blankets.

00:28:42.679 --> 00:28:47.267
The blankets that came from the
Lesotho people of South Africa.

00:28:47.267 --> 00:28:51.980
And how we printed
vibranium on one side.

00:28:51.980 --> 00:28:57.402
And when Marvel saw these
blankets, they like them.

00:28:57.402 --> 00:28:59.779
But they thought
they were too heavy.

00:28:59.779 --> 00:29:01.656
And so at the last
minute, we had

00:29:01.656 --> 00:29:06.077
to shave all the blankets
with a men's razor, just

00:29:06.077 --> 00:29:07.495
to make them thinner.

00:29:07.495 --> 00:29:10.290
And we had about 150 blankets.

00:29:10.290 --> 00:29:13.668
Then we sent a
video to Marvel that

00:29:13.668 --> 00:29:17.297
would show how these
blankets actually now

00:29:17.297 --> 00:29:19.924
are thinner after
their shave and they

00:29:19.924 --> 00:29:22.302
have their vibranium on them.

00:29:22.302 --> 00:29:24.053
So they are shields.

00:29:24.053 --> 00:29:28.057
And once they saw our video,
they approved the blankets.

00:29:30.852 --> 00:29:35.190
The Dora Milaje, the
highest ranking female force

00:29:35.190 --> 00:29:36.649
in Wakanda.

00:29:36.649 --> 00:29:39.778
Dora Milaje means
the adorned ones.

00:29:39.778 --> 00:29:44.449
And these young women are
the King's bodyguards.

00:29:44.449 --> 00:29:47.744
Here's how we created
their costume.

00:29:47.744 --> 00:29:50.830
On your right see the
costume illustration

00:29:50.830 --> 00:29:56.419
that was done by Anthony
Francesco of the Marvel design

00:29:56.419 --> 00:29:57.462
group.

00:29:57.462 --> 00:29:59.380
On your left, you
see the comics.

00:29:59.380 --> 00:30:01.299
And they're quite
different, their costumes

00:30:01.299 --> 00:30:03.551
are quite different
in the comics.

00:30:03.551 --> 00:30:06.346
And that's because Ryan
Coogler our director

00:30:06.346 --> 00:30:12.101
really wanted them to be
seen seriously in uniform.

00:30:12.101 --> 00:30:17.899
And all of their vitals covered,
because they're fighters.

00:30:17.899 --> 00:30:22.070
Not cheerleaders
skirts, and bikini tops,

00:30:22.070 --> 00:30:27.534
but actual costume
design that would

00:30:27.534 --> 00:30:31.663
represent many areas of Africa.

00:30:31.663 --> 00:30:33.498
So we started with the Maasai.

00:30:33.498 --> 00:30:36.918
And I thought we need
to bring up the red.

00:30:36.918 --> 00:30:42.131
We need to show this beautiful,
vibrant red of the Maasai.

00:30:42.131 --> 00:30:44.551
And then we went to the Himba.

00:30:44.551 --> 00:30:48.304
The Himba women who adorn
themselves with the shea

00:30:48.304 --> 00:30:49.597
butter and the clay.

00:30:49.597 --> 00:30:52.517
And they put it in their
hair and on their bodies.

00:30:52.517 --> 00:30:54.686
And also they take
the cat skins,

00:30:54.686 --> 00:31:00.024
and they stretch, and adorn the
cat skins, and create costumes.

00:31:00.024 --> 00:31:01.943
We took all of those elements.

00:31:01.943 --> 00:31:05.780
And we returned the illustration
that was done by Anthony.

00:31:05.780 --> 00:31:07.323
We turned it forward.

00:31:07.323 --> 00:31:11.077
We added all of these
elements to this costume

00:31:11.077 --> 00:31:13.121
including beadwork.

00:31:13.121 --> 00:31:17.667
And as you know bead work
is a very, very common

00:31:17.667 --> 00:31:21.087
artistic element of Africa.

00:31:21.087 --> 00:31:23.423
And has several meanings.

00:31:23.423 --> 00:31:27.343
So this is how our
costume came about.

00:31:27.343 --> 00:31:32.807
We created a tabard, we
created the Himba leather

00:31:32.807 --> 00:31:39.105
that centered around the body
and honored the female form.

00:31:39.105 --> 00:31:42.108
Also there was a back
skirt that the leather

00:31:42.108 --> 00:31:45.278
was stretched and
adorned in the same way

00:31:45.278 --> 00:31:47.071
that the Himba women do.

00:31:47.071 --> 00:31:51.701
The Turkana tribe was our
inspiration for the beadwork.

00:31:51.701 --> 00:31:56.122
And we also had scarification.

00:31:56.122 --> 00:31:59.876
So we wanted to show
different ways, different ways

00:31:59.876 --> 00:32:02.337
of showing beauty.

00:32:02.337 --> 00:32:06.090
Scarification is
a form of beauty.

00:32:06.090 --> 00:32:09.802
And we took the neck rings
and the shoulder armor

00:32:09.802 --> 00:32:12.639
and we put a high sheen on them.

00:32:12.639 --> 00:32:15.350
We plated them so they
would look like jewelry.

00:32:17.936 --> 00:32:23.858
And this is a Okoye as the
Dora, the leader of the Dora.

00:32:23.858 --> 00:32:29.822
So you see how you can
take culture and apply it

00:32:29.822 --> 00:32:32.325
to a superhero model.

00:32:32.325 --> 00:32:35.411
And that is called Afro future.

00:32:38.456 --> 00:32:41.834
I mentioned in Mbaku who is the
fierce Warrior of the Jabari

00:32:41.834 --> 00:32:42.460
tribe.

00:32:42.460 --> 00:32:44.921
You might remember
him in the film.

00:32:44.921 --> 00:32:47.799
And he says we are vegetarians.

00:32:47.799 --> 00:32:54.055
Well, we wanted to honor him and
his character from the comics

00:32:54.055 --> 00:32:56.975
in a beautiful way.

00:32:56.975 --> 00:33:02.021
They honor the
silverback gorilla.

00:33:02.021 --> 00:33:05.316
And then the comics
he has a gorilla

00:33:05.316 --> 00:33:11.864
face that is very derogatory
and a sign of the times.

00:33:11.864 --> 00:33:16.160
So we decided to give him
a more honorable costume.

00:33:16.160 --> 00:33:20.540
And we put the silverback
fur on his back like a cape.

00:33:20.540 --> 00:33:23.960
And the cape was
silvertipped a very hard

00:33:23.960 --> 00:33:25.878
to stay on his shoulders.

00:33:25.878 --> 00:33:27.505
We tried everything.

00:33:27.505 --> 00:33:31.009
And the only thing that
would keep that cape up there

00:33:31.009 --> 00:33:33.261
was refrigerator magnets.

00:33:33.261 --> 00:33:35.763
And then once we put
the refrigerator magnets

00:33:35.763 --> 00:33:40.935
and it stuck, it stayed and
it was very hard to get off.

00:33:40.935 --> 00:33:44.897
But they are inspired
by the Dogan of Mali.

00:33:44.897 --> 00:33:45.857
The Dogan.

00:33:45.857 --> 00:33:47.859
They were one of the
first astronomers

00:33:47.859 --> 00:33:52.030
and they have a celebration
to the solstice.

00:33:52.030 --> 00:33:56.826
Once Ryan Coogler saw
images of their celebration

00:33:56.826 --> 00:33:59.829
and their grass
skirts, he thought then

00:33:59.829 --> 00:34:02.623
the Jabari should
wear grass skirts.

00:34:02.623 --> 00:34:06.586
And this is how we started
our creation of the grass

00:34:06.586 --> 00:34:12.216
skirt for Mbaku who was the
leader of the Jabari tribe.

00:34:12.216 --> 00:34:16.220
We sent this video
to Ryan and to Marvel

00:34:16.220 --> 00:34:19.766
to show how the grass
skirt would work.

00:34:24.103 --> 00:34:25.813
And it was approved.

00:34:25.813 --> 00:34:29.817
This is how it played
out at the Warrior falls.

00:34:29.817 --> 00:34:33.529
Shuri who is this little
sister to T'challa

00:34:33.529 --> 00:34:35.698
creates the Black Panther suit.

00:34:35.698 --> 00:34:37.366
So she works in a lab.

00:34:37.366 --> 00:34:40.620
And because of her youth
I did want her costume

00:34:40.620 --> 00:34:45.374
to be like a lab coat
and boring, because she's

00:34:45.374 --> 00:34:47.627
so young and so ingenious.

00:34:47.627 --> 00:34:49.295
So we gave her overlays.

00:34:49.295 --> 00:34:52.840
Most of her costumes
had wonderful overlays

00:34:52.840 --> 00:34:56.511
like the organza sweater
you see on your left

00:34:56.511 --> 00:34:59.931
and the sheer netting that
you see on your right.

00:34:59.931 --> 00:35:02.975
And those were her
protective layers.

00:35:02.975 --> 00:35:08.773
So we created Afro future with
the Black Panther costumes.

00:35:08.773 --> 00:35:12.693
We took technology
and we infused it

00:35:12.693 --> 00:35:14.987
in a superhero model.

00:35:14.987 --> 00:35:17.740
And this is how it
all came together.

00:35:27.125 --> 00:35:31.629
Thank you Ruth, so much for
that amazing presentation.

00:35:31.629 --> 00:35:33.881
At this time we're going
to take a few questions

00:35:33.881 --> 00:35:36.801
from some students.

00:35:36.801 --> 00:35:41.973
In what part of the film process
does the costume designer

00:35:41.973 --> 00:35:47.019
start to get involved with
the film production process?

00:35:47.019 --> 00:35:51.524
It really depends
on what director,

00:35:51.524 --> 00:35:53.025
what the director wants.

00:35:53.025 --> 00:35:57.280
When I work with Spike Lee,
he sends me the script way

00:35:57.280 --> 00:36:00.867
before we start our process.

00:36:00.867 --> 00:36:05.288
He really wants me to start
thinking about these characters

00:36:05.288 --> 00:36:06.956
early.

00:36:06.956 --> 00:36:11.210
And so I feel like I'm
one of the first people

00:36:11.210 --> 00:36:15.298
to get started right in line
with the production designer.

00:36:15.298 --> 00:36:18.384
But on Black Panther, I was--

00:36:18.384 --> 00:36:23.055
I came in to Marvel I've never
done a superhero film before.

00:36:23.055 --> 00:36:25.349
And Marvel is like the CIA.

00:36:25.349 --> 00:36:28.269
And everything is top secret.

00:36:28.269 --> 00:36:32.899
And I met with Ryan Coogler
and Hannah Bechler and Met More

00:36:32.899 --> 00:36:36.277
who was the producer and
the production designer.

00:36:36.277 --> 00:36:38.863
So they were on
long before I was.

00:36:38.863 --> 00:36:43.242
And as I walk into the office I
saw images all over the walls.

00:36:43.242 --> 00:36:45.953
And I saw development in place.

00:36:45.953 --> 00:36:49.373
And I really felt like
I needed to catch up.

00:36:49.373 --> 00:36:53.252
So it's nice when you can
come on as early as possible.

00:36:53.252 --> 00:36:57.798
Sometimes in independent
films you come on much earlier

00:36:57.798 --> 00:37:01.177
than you might in a studio film.

00:37:01.177 --> 00:37:03.304
OK.

00:37:03.304 --> 00:37:08.309
What would you say
are some skills needed

00:37:08.309 --> 00:37:11.938
for being a costumer
for film and television?

00:37:15.191 --> 00:37:19.528
You have to be able to
imagine the characters

00:37:19.528 --> 00:37:24.533
and see them in a way
that's achievable.

00:37:24.533 --> 00:37:29.372
So if we said Oh, I'm imagining
these characters flying in

00:37:29.372 --> 00:37:34.126
on a spaceship and getting
out and their costumes morph

00:37:34.126 --> 00:37:37.964
into different things, you might
not be able to achieve that.

00:37:37.964 --> 00:37:41.801
So when you read
your script, you're

00:37:41.801 --> 00:37:47.390
thinking about you're
imagining this world.

00:37:47.390 --> 00:37:53.604
And you're imagining it in a
way that you can achieve it.

00:37:53.604 --> 00:37:56.565
And it's not to say
that you shouldn't think

00:37:56.565 --> 00:37:58.776
outside the box,
and it shouldn't

00:37:58.776 --> 00:38:01.862
bring innovative ideas into it.

00:38:01.862 --> 00:38:04.573
Because maybe that
spaceship idea

00:38:04.573 --> 00:38:07.451
morphs and changes
as you go along.

00:38:07.451 --> 00:38:09.287
And as you read
your script again.

00:38:09.287 --> 00:38:12.164
And as you start doing
your illustrations.

00:38:12.164 --> 00:38:14.875
But at some point you
have to really take

00:38:14.875 --> 00:38:21.465
imagination and research and
apply it to a real 3D form.

00:38:21.465 --> 00:38:23.759
You've got to create
these characters.

00:38:23.759 --> 00:38:29.849
And so I think imagination
is very important.

00:38:29.849 --> 00:38:32.393
I agree, love that.

00:38:32.393 --> 00:38:38.149
How did you, in the
film Black Panther,

00:38:38.149 --> 00:38:41.902
how did you stay true
to the comic book

00:38:41.902 --> 00:38:45.323
and true to the African
culture while you

00:38:45.323 --> 00:38:48.868
were creating the movie?

00:38:48.868 --> 00:38:50.036
Yes.

00:38:50.036 --> 00:38:55.666
Well, the comics actually
did provide a free space,

00:38:55.666 --> 00:38:56.709
a safe space.

00:38:56.709 --> 00:38:58.794
Because when you
look at the comics,

00:38:58.794 --> 00:39:03.174
you saw Masai you saw in the
Ndebele, you saw Turkana,

00:39:03.174 --> 00:39:06.844
you saw all the tribes
of Africa represented.

00:39:06.844 --> 00:39:10.598
So I was joyous when I
looked at the comics,

00:39:10.598 --> 00:39:15.061
because it gave me an
entry to the creativity

00:39:15.061 --> 00:39:17.021
that I was going to experience.

00:39:17.021 --> 00:39:20.649
But they're little tiny
boxes, they're small boxes.

00:39:20.649 --> 00:39:23.778
So you so detail is not there.

00:39:23.778 --> 00:39:26.238
So you get a general
and broad sense,

00:39:26.238 --> 00:39:29.867
but a good sense of what
will Wakanda should be like.

00:39:29.867 --> 00:39:32.286
And so it was an
opportunity for me

00:39:32.286 --> 00:39:34.872
when I applied it
to the costumes

00:39:34.872 --> 00:39:39.001
to show all the majesty,
all the detail, all

00:39:39.001 --> 00:39:40.461
the different areas.

00:39:40.461 --> 00:39:45.591
It was like a love letter,
an open book to creativity.

00:39:45.591 --> 00:39:48.552
Nice.

00:39:48.552 --> 00:39:53.182
Can you explain a time that
you had to make a hard decision

00:39:53.182 --> 00:39:59.438
or fight for your point of
view to be seen or heard?

00:39:59.438 --> 00:40:00.898
Yes.

00:40:00.898 --> 00:40:04.527
You have to trust your voice.

00:40:04.527 --> 00:40:09.198
You have to feel
things in your heart.

00:40:09.198 --> 00:40:12.827
But as artists sometimes
we have to let go,

00:40:12.827 --> 00:40:16.956
because this is a
collaborative medium.

00:40:16.956 --> 00:40:20.209
It's not that you're on
your own island doing

00:40:20.209 --> 00:40:22.878
everything your way
and everybody else

00:40:22.878 --> 00:40:24.505
is in a separate area.

00:40:24.505 --> 00:40:27.049
We're really actually
collaborating.

00:40:27.049 --> 00:40:30.553
And so if you open
your mind to things

00:40:30.553 --> 00:40:36.016
that may not agree to what
you thought it would be,

00:40:36.016 --> 00:40:38.352
actually you might
learn something.

00:40:38.352 --> 00:40:40.479
You might learn something new.

00:40:40.479 --> 00:40:45.109
And the new idea might
open up an avenue that's

00:40:45.109 --> 00:40:47.528
even bigger than the first one.

00:40:47.528 --> 00:40:53.576
So I always say be open to the
ideas of your collaborators,

00:40:53.576 --> 00:41:00.082
because every time someone hears
a note, or they read a story,

00:41:00.082 --> 00:41:04.128
or they listen to
what's going on,

00:41:04.128 --> 00:41:06.755
each person will
interpret it differently,

00:41:06.755 --> 00:41:09.800
each person will hear
something different.

00:41:09.800 --> 00:41:15.681
So not getting your way or
not having your special idea

00:41:15.681 --> 00:41:18.350
in the film is not terrible.

00:41:18.350 --> 00:41:21.103
You can store it and
use it on another film,

00:41:21.103 --> 00:41:23.814
or use it in another way.

00:41:23.814 --> 00:41:29.361
But the collaboration is
a bigger payoff for you.

00:41:29.361 --> 00:41:31.572
Nice.

00:41:31.572 --> 00:41:35.868
What part of costuming
brings you the most joy?

00:41:39.538 --> 00:41:44.919
I think, I get very
excited about culture.

00:41:44.919 --> 00:41:50.174
And when I first started out
and I was doing Malcolm X.

00:41:50.174 --> 00:41:52.801
I went to the Department
of Corrections,

00:41:52.801 --> 00:41:55.930
and I got his files
where he was in prison

00:41:55.930 --> 00:41:58.307
for a short time
of his life, where

00:41:58.307 --> 00:42:00.017
it was a transition for him.

00:42:00.017 --> 00:42:02.895
And I wanted to know more
about that transition.

00:42:02.895 --> 00:42:06.565
And so I was excited to read
his letters that he wrote.

00:42:06.565 --> 00:42:09.610
I saw his whole file
his whole history.

00:42:09.610 --> 00:42:12.071
And it was firsthand from him.

00:42:12.071 --> 00:42:16.742
It was like he was
telling me about his life.

00:42:16.742 --> 00:42:20.955
Also on Amistad I wondered
how these Africans

00:42:20.955 --> 00:42:25.834
from Sierra Leone how it was
what it was like for them

00:42:25.834 --> 00:42:26.961
on that ship.

00:42:26.961 --> 00:42:33.342
And so I had no other writings
except for the cargo list.

00:42:33.342 --> 00:42:36.262
What was the ship carrying?

00:42:36.262 --> 00:42:39.598
Because they were carrying
not only Africans,

00:42:39.598 --> 00:42:45.187
they were carrying goods,
blankets, materials, food.

00:42:45.187 --> 00:42:49.358
So when they mutinied the
ship will adorn themselves.

00:42:49.358 --> 00:42:51.235
They're going to
put those blankets

00:42:51.235 --> 00:42:54.321
on their bodies the way that
they would in their homeland.

00:42:54.321 --> 00:42:57.950
It was we were already in
America, in the Eastern

00:42:57.950 --> 00:42:59.952
seaboard, it was cold.

00:42:59.952 --> 00:43:02.288
They would wrap their heads.

00:43:02.288 --> 00:43:04.290
So that's how I told
their story and I

00:43:04.290 --> 00:43:08.794
get very excited about
the research portion that

00:43:08.794 --> 00:43:14.258
gives me a clue to the real
story, the truth of the story.

00:43:14.258 --> 00:43:16.302
What could have really happened?

00:43:19.638 --> 00:43:24.310
What would you say is your
greatest achievement thus

00:43:24.310 --> 00:43:27.730
far for you?

00:43:27.730 --> 00:43:32.234
My greatest achievement
thus far for me

00:43:32.234 --> 00:43:38.657
is not only winning
the Oscar, but also

00:43:38.657 --> 00:43:46.915
being told that people of
Africa loved Black Panther.

00:43:46.915 --> 00:43:50.836
And I was representing their
culture in costume design,

00:43:50.836 --> 00:43:55.591
and not being African
- of African descent.

00:43:55.591 --> 00:44:01.388
And when we go through our
research in the history books,

00:44:01.388 --> 00:44:04.892
it's so easy to misrepresent.

00:44:04.892 --> 00:44:10.898
And so the accomplishment
of authentic representation

00:44:10.898 --> 00:44:13.442
means the most to me.

00:44:13.442 --> 00:44:15.569
Nice.

00:44:15.569 --> 00:44:18.155
When creating Black
Panther, did you

00:44:18.155 --> 00:44:21.700
get a chance to actually
visit the continent of Africa?

00:44:21.700 --> 00:44:25.871
And if so, what
part did you visit?

00:44:25.871 --> 00:44:32.127
Well, I went to Africa
after we shot Black Panther.

00:44:32.127 --> 00:44:36.382
I researched the tribes
through the history books,

00:44:36.382 --> 00:44:40.344
because we were
basing the costumes

00:44:40.344 --> 00:44:45.349
on the ancient indigenous
tribal customs and technologies

00:44:45.349 --> 00:44:47.017
that they were using.

00:44:47.017 --> 00:44:51.605
And bringing it into
a superhero form.

00:44:51.605 --> 00:44:54.566
So if I had actually
gone to Africa

00:44:54.566 --> 00:44:56.819
to research all
of those details,

00:44:56.819 --> 00:44:58.821
I'd still be there right now.

00:44:58.821 --> 00:45:03.701
But I had known so much by
the end of Black Panther

00:45:03.701 --> 00:45:08.247
when I did my vacation,
I went to Johannesburg

00:45:08.247 --> 00:45:12.167
to the first Afropunk
celebration there.

00:45:12.167 --> 00:45:17.881
I went to Cape Town and I just
laid back and enjoy myself.

00:45:17.881 --> 00:45:23.303
And then I went up into the
bush on a private Safari.

00:45:23.303 --> 00:45:28.142
And I immersed myself
again in the culture

00:45:28.142 --> 00:45:30.102
and had a beautiful time.

00:45:30.102 --> 00:45:33.897
I was there for like six weeks.

00:45:33.897 --> 00:45:35.399
Nice, nice.

00:45:35.399 --> 00:45:38.819
And this is going to
be our last question.

00:45:38.819 --> 00:45:42.030
Are there any
resources, books, films,

00:45:42.030 --> 00:45:46.243
that you would recommend
to a future costumer, that

00:45:46.243 --> 00:45:49.288
would help them learn
storyboarding, character, tone,

00:45:49.288 --> 00:45:51.790
and mood?

00:45:51.790 --> 00:45:56.128
I think first there's
a couple of programs

00:45:56.128 --> 00:45:59.840
that are good to learn one's
called Costume Plot Pro.

00:45:59.840 --> 00:46:08.015
Another one is called Sync
On-set and those are tools.

00:46:08.015 --> 00:46:10.851
As far as breaking
down costume design,

00:46:10.851 --> 00:46:15.856
it's good to look
into costume books.

00:46:15.856 --> 00:46:18.650
There's one called
Hollywood Costume.

00:46:18.650 --> 00:46:23.405
There's another one
called What They Wore.

00:46:23.405 --> 00:46:27.493
You can Google things
and get basic knowledge

00:46:27.493 --> 00:46:29.369
of costume design.

00:46:29.369 --> 00:46:36.084
But character, there's no
manual for the imagination that

00:46:36.084 --> 00:46:38.754
is involved when
you're coming up

00:46:38.754 --> 00:46:41.215
with a costume design
for a character.

00:46:41.215 --> 00:46:45.469
There is no roadmap, it's
completely subjective.

00:46:45.469 --> 00:46:49.640
It's whatever you
experienced in your life

00:46:49.640 --> 00:46:51.809
and you think that this
character should be.

00:46:51.809 --> 00:46:57.731
So I would read,
I would observe,

00:46:57.731 --> 00:47:02.152
I would live your life,
and be a people person.

00:47:02.152 --> 00:47:05.781
Understand what makes
people, make the decisions

00:47:05.781 --> 00:47:07.074
that they make.

00:47:07.074 --> 00:47:10.410
And what happens as they
go through their lives,

00:47:10.410 --> 00:47:14.832
you really do have to love
people and their differences.

00:47:14.832 --> 00:47:20.462
Awesome I would like to
thank you first hand for one,

00:47:20.462 --> 00:47:25.467
being my mentor and thank
the Academy for matching us

00:47:25.467 --> 00:47:26.593
together.

00:47:26.593 --> 00:47:29.054
And having this
conversation with me.

00:47:29.054 --> 00:47:32.057
So just thank you for your time.

00:47:32.057 --> 00:47:34.226
Thank you LaShell.

00:47:34.226 --> 00:47:40.315
I enjoyed taking you through
the creation of Black Panther.

00:47:40.315 --> 00:47:42.609
And a little bit of my story.

00:47:42.609 --> 00:47:47.614
And hopefully it will inspire
you to create costume design

00:47:47.614 --> 00:47:49.074
for film.

00:47:49.074 --> 00:47:54.621
I enjoy infusing African culture
into the superhero model.

00:47:54.621 --> 00:47:58.917
I enjoy being a
messenger of authenticity

00:47:58.917 --> 00:48:03.714
with tribal, culture,
and modern technology.

00:48:03.714 --> 00:48:06.425
So good luck to you.

00:48:06.425 --> 00:48:10.012
I wish you the best
and Wakanda forever.

